    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1263">

                <pb facs="1144" n="886" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg886"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 811.741/155</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The</hi>
                    
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Secretary of State</hi>
                    
                    <hi rendition="#italic">to the British Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Spring
                        Rice</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 749]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-04-21">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 21, 1915</hi>
                        </date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Excellency</hi>: Referring to your note of March 26,
                    1915, with regard to the request of the Governor of British Honduras for
                    information as to whether this Government would be willing to permit the sending
                    of commercial messages by radio from that colony to addresses in the United
                    States, through New Orleans, in code, under certain restrictions, I have the
                    honor to inform you that this Government deems it inadvisable to grant the
                    permission desired by the Governor of British Honduras. The transmission of
                    commercial code messages between the United States radio stations and radio
                    stations in foreign belligerent countries has not been permitted even in the
                    case of the Tuckerton station operated under control of the Navy Department, and
                    it is not considered advisable to make an exception to this rule in the case of
                    radio stations in belligerent territory.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">W. J. Bryan</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
